hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  one of the puzzles that might arise for  a first-time reader of John Stuart  Mill's treatise utilitarianism is given  that he makes this distinction between  higher and lower pleasures why would  anybody anybody in their right mind  anybody who is experienced or perceptive  prefer lower pleasures to higher  pleasures and we want to keep in mind  first what does mill actually mean by  higher and lower pleasures so with  qualitative utilitarianism he says that  there's a distinction that we can make  between pleasures and also a way of life  that engages our higher faculties the  things that make us distinctively human  our intellectual faculties our moral  faculties he also talks about our  aesthetic appreciation of things and  that goes beyond what he calls  sensibility or  or you know the pleasures of the senses  this also applies to pain as well there  can be qualitatively worse pains and  qualitatively lesser pains here we're  more concerned with the issue of  pleasure and the way that Mill describes  it is that how do we know that that  something is a higher pleasure well we  look at those who are actually  experienced in both types of pleasures  and we see which one they elevate and  which one they put lower the way that we  actually do this is we look to see which  one they prefer in situations where they  can't have both of them because if they  can have both of them Mill sees no  problem with that as a matter of fact  from utilitarian perspective if we can  enjoy every pleasure possible that's  that's wonderful  now we often do have to make choices  prioritizations even sacrifices and then  Mill says that in a case like that  the ideal judge the person who really is  in touch with the values of the  pleasures will prefer the higher to the  lower pleasures so given the choice  between for example books and beer  if you can't have both well then you're  gonna prefer books because that engages  your intellectual faculties given the  chance to go out and have a nice dinner  party with your friends or to engage in  some socially beneficial activity you  will engage in the socially beneficial  activity because that engages your moral  faculties and we can go on and on and on  so then the question is okay granting  that why would anybody once they  experience pleasures why would they ever  prefer as it seems most people indeed do  lower pleasures to higher pleasures and  Mill has some some good answers for this  and he he considers two objections early  on in in Chapter two of utilitarianism  so one objection is that we do see  people as he says postpone a higher  pleasure for a lower pleasure they they  go for the lower pleasure which is you  know right there in front of them and  then that means that they have to put it  off engaging in a higher pleasure what  might be an example of this you know I'm  set up to read some John Stuart Mill  which should be an intellectual activity  should be quite pleasurable because he's  actually a good writer not like his  predecessor Jeremy Bentham who's very  dry to read but Mill is quite  interesting so I'm ready to do this and  then I get you know distracted by  something else I start perhaps watching  a show a comedy show on Netflix and it's  not a particularly intellectually  stimulating one it's just kind of a  goofy one and and I get sucked into this  this thing where I watch show after show  after show until I fall asleep and the  book never gets opened up now why does  that sort of thing happen what mill has  an explanation for this he says that  here we go many who are capable of the  higher pleasures occasionally under the  influence of temptation postpone them to  the lower but this is quite compatible  with a full appreciation of  intrinsic superiority of the higher men  often from infirmity of character make  their election for the nearer good  whatever is easier to acquire whatever  is closer in time reading a book right  you have to get into it and it takes a  bit of time to figure out what's being  said so he says they know that this is  less valuable and this no less when the  choice is between two bodily pleasures  and when it is between bodily and mental  so they pursue central indulgences to  the injury of health though perfectly  aware that health is the greater good  people do this in terms of bodily  pleasures why can't they do it in terms  of bodily and mental pleasures it makes  perfect sense people again do this all  of the time just think of it you know  for his example how many people sign up  for gym memberships that they will never  actually use beyond say the first week  this is how gyms make their money they  think oh this dynamic similarly we may  find that people say that they're going  to do something that is intellectually  stimulating or morally valuable or you  know aesthetically pleasing as opposed  to merely sensibly pleasing and then  they go for the the sensual pleasures  that happens the other objection is is  in some respects more interesting he  says what about younger people who early  on in their life they have in fact been  introduced to the higher pleasures and  they do pursue them the pleasures of the  mind the pleasures of the human heart  you might say and then as time goes on  as they get older something changes they  shift they they're no longer interested  in those and they focus on the sensual  pleasures so he says this is can be  understood just as well he says I don't  believe that those who undergo this very  common change he's saying that it is  common voluntarily choose the lower  description of pleasures in preference  to the higher so what's really going on  there he tells us I believe that before  they devote themselves exclude  to the one they've already become  incapable of the other it says capacity  for the noble feelings is in most  nature's a very tender plant easily  killed not just by hostile influences  things coming in from the outside but  from we mere want of sustenance and in  the majority of young persons it  speedily dies away if the occupations to  which their position and life has  devoted them and the society in which  has thrown them are not favourable to  keeping that in exercise so although we  may start out with good experience of  both the higher and the lower pleasures  unless we're actively using our mental  faculties unless we're getting the sort  of stimulation that we need and and  perhaps even engaging in some deliberate  reflection and choices to do something  with it it's going to wither away and in  a case like that the person shifts from  the higher to the lower pleasures  because they no longer appreciate the  higher pleasures as higher pleasures  what is the sign of that the very fact  that they do in fact prefer the lower  that they tell you why say sitting and  and drinking in front of the fireplace  is preferable to cracking open a book or  having an intellectual conversation or  going out and doing some you know  strenuous but rewarding activities or  listening to a symphony they'd rather  just let you know Netflix play on and on  and on while they sit there drinking  some some wine or beer or cocktails or  whatever right so you know these sort of  cases show us that Mill does have some  explanation for how people lose or don't  actuate their capacity for these higher  pleasures and then end up preferring  lower  there's also another interesting issue  which is as he sees it a major problem  with society of his time and I think you  could say that from utilitarian  perspective it's also a major problem in  our contemporary society which is that  most people do not have moral and  intellectual development fostered within  their life which makes it impossible for  them to appreciate the higher pleasures  because they never had a shot at it in  the first place he talks about you know  people say a lot of people are willing  to sacrifice you know pleasure happiness  he says yeah nineteen twentieth of the  population routinely have to do this  because of the kind of work that they're  stuck doing so what would afford the  possibility for people to not skip in  and not miss out on the development of  their intellectual and moral faculties  well education would have to play a role  in that and you might say also a  reorganization of society including the  the economy interestingly this probably  applies almost as much in our own time  as it did in Mills own time in the heart  of the Industrial Revolution when many  people were sinking into well you know  what later would be called the  proletariat or working-class and many  people were working very late hours  children were you know getting drawn  into the workplace rather than being  afforded the possibility for education  even if we have educational institutions  they may not be of the sort that do in  fact lead to effective moral and  intellectual development in which case  mill would say from utilitarian  perspective what is really desirable is  to rework them so that that does become  a possibility for everybody so he's  talking about the opportunity for human  development distinctively  human development not merely to satisfy  lower pleasures which are in fact  pleasures he's not saying though they're  bad at all but to afford the possibility  of higher pleasures this becomes a duty  from a utilitarian perspective if we  take quality into account  you
 

